/E (The vehicle's controller is a Raspberry Pi 5 \(8GB\). We started on the 4GB model but switched to the 8GB version after finding the 4GB board too slow, causing noticeable lag during camera processing. It reads the camera and ultrasonic sensors, decides steering/driving commands, and sends them to the actuators described in Section 2. It was chosen for the CPU power needed to run our camera-based wall-percentage detection and traffic-sign recognition in real time, while still fitting within the power budget of the 5V powerbank.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [7]
>>
